# Copy to merchant/.env. That file is gitignored, and it should stay that way: an
# Admin token is a privileged credential for the store and scopes it was granted.
# Never put real credentials in this file.
#
# There are two ways to run this example, and this file is where you choose between them.
# The first needs nothing at all; the second needs a store.
#
# The token, when there is one, is read once — in api/agent_config.py — and handed to the
# Admin GraphQL client. It is never sent to the model, returned by a route, written to a
# log, or included in an error message.

# ── 1. The local store ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to 1 and every variable below becomes optional: the example runs against
# api/local_store.py, an in-process stand-in for one store's Admin API, seeded from
# data/seed.json. No Shopify account, no token, no network. A staged change still stages,
# the approval gate still holds it, and an approved change really does change the catalog
# the portal is reading — the whole path is exercised, and nothing leaves the machine.
#
# What it cannot stand in for: sessions and conversion (no local store can know them, so
# traffic reads 0 and the agent is told so), the real schema, scopes, and anything a real
# store would reject. Those are what the second option is for.
SHOPIFY_LOCAL_STORE=1

# ── 2. A Shopify development store ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comment out SHOPIFY_LOCAL_STORE above, then fill in these two. The README's Run section
# has the steps: create a development store, create an app for it in the Shopify Dev
# Dashboard, and mint an Admin API access token with that app's client ID and secret.

# The store, without a scheme: acme-supply.myshopify.com
SHOPIFY_SHOP_DOMAIN=

# An Admin API access token (shpat_…) for that store. One minted from a client ID and secret
# expires after 24 hours, and minting another is one request; the README's Run section has it,
# lists the scopes to put on the app, and says what stops working without each one.
SHOPIFY_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# Pinned Admin GraphQL version. Leave unset to use the version the example was written
# against (api/agent_config.py names it).
SHOPIFY_ADMIN_API_VERSION=

# ── Either way ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The chat credential. Its home is the repo-root .env (see .env.example there), which this
# example also reads; setting it here keeps everything this example needs in one file.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Who the change ledger stamps on staged and applied changes. In a real deployment this
# comes from the host's own authentication, not from configuration.
SHOPIFY_OPERATOR=Avery

# Display name for the portal. The shop's own name is used when this is unset.
SHOPIFY_STORE_NAME=

# Alert thresholds: store-wide defaults. Per-type and per-product entries in
# data/thresholds.json take precedence over these.
SHOPIFY_LOW_STOCK_DEFAULT=8
SHOPIFY_FULFILMENT_SLA_DAYS=3

# Set to 1 to skip ShopifyQL and derive every metric from the order scan. Worth doing when
# the token has no read_reports scope, or to see the fallback path on a store that does —
# including the local one, which answers the sales half of ShopifyQL.
SHOPIFY_DISABLE_SHOPIFYQL=0

# Set to 0 to let the assistant apply its own changes without an approval recorded by the
# portal. Leave it on: the approval gate is the point of the example.
MERCHANT_REQUIRE_HOST_APPROVAL=1
